.TH FREE 1 2026-10-19 "w64devkit" "w64devkit Manual"
.SH NAME
free \- show the amount of free and used memory
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B free
.RB [ \-b | \-k | \-m | \-g | \-h ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B free
prints the amount of physical memory and paging file space in the system
on standard output.
It takes a single sample and exits.
.PP
The output is three lines: a header, a
.B Mem:
line for physical memory, and a
.B Swap:
line for the paging files.
Each line starts with its label padded to 7 columns, which is blank in
the header, and then has fields that are 12 characters wide and right
aligned.
A value too wide for its field is not truncated, and pushes the rest of
the line to the right.
The header names six columns:
.BR total ,
.BR used ,
.BR free ,
.BR shared ,
.BR buff/cache ,
and
.BR available .
The
.B Mem:
line has a value for each, and the
.B Swap:
line has only the first three.
.PP
The columns of the
.B Mem:
line are:
.TP
.B total
The physical memory in the system.
.TP
.B used
The total less
.B free
and
.BR buff/cache .
.TP
.B free
The physical memory that Windows reports as available, less
.BR buff/cache .
It is 0 when the system cache is at least as large as the available
memory.
Otherwise the sum of
.B free
and
.B buff/cache
is the memory that Windows reports as available.
.TP
.B shared
Always 0.
.TP
.B buff/cache
The size of the system cache.
.TP
.B available
Always 0.
This port has no value for it.
.PP
The columns of the
.B Swap:
line are:
.TP
.B total
The sum of the sizes of the paging files.
.TP
.B used
The sum of the space in use in the paging files.
.TP
.B free
The total less the space in use.
.PP
All three are 0 when there is no paging file.
.PP
Sizes are in kibibytes (units of 1024 bytes), unless an option selects
another unit.
Each size is the number of bytes divided by the unit and rounded to the
nearest integer, with halves rounded up.
A size smaller than half a unit is shown as 0, and the rounded columns
may not add up.
.PP
With
.BR \-h ,
each size is scaled by powers of 1024 to the largest unit that keeps it
below 1024, and written with one decimal digit and a suffix:
.BR K ,
.BR M ,
.BR G ,
or
.BR T .
For example, 33271988 KiB is written
.BR 31.7G .
A size of 0 is written as a plain
.BR 0 ,
with no suffix.
.SH OPTIONS
Only the first argument is examined, and only if it begins with a dash.
The character after the dash selects the option, and any further
characters in the argument are ignored, so
.B \-mk
is the same as
.BR \-m ,
and
.B \-bytes
is the same as
.BR \-b .
Options cannot be grouped, a second option has no effect, and arguments
that follow the first are ignored.
A first argument that does not begin with a dash is ignored too.
Any other first argument that begins with a dash, including a lone
.B \-
and
.B \-\-
but not
.BR \-\-help ,
prints a usage message on standard error and exits with status 1.
.TP
.B \-b
Show sizes in bytes.
.TP
.B \-k
Show sizes in kibibytes.
This is the default.
.TP
.B \-m
Show sizes in mebibytes (1048576 bytes).
.TP
.B \-g
Show sizes in gibibytes (1073741824 bytes).
.TP
.B \-h
Show sizes in a human-readable form, as described above.
It does not print a usage message.
.TP
.B \-\-help
Print a usage message on standard output and exit with status 0.
It is recognized only as the first argument, and any arguments that
follow are ignored.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.B free
reads no environment variables.
The variables that busybox reads for every applet, such as
.BR BB_GLOBBING ,
are described in
.BR busybox (1).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success, or
.B \-\-help
was given.
.TP
.B 1
Invalid option.
.PP
Failure to read a statistic is not an error.
A value that the system does not supply is shown as 0.
.SH EXAMPLES
Show sizes in a human-readable form:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ free \-h
.EE
.RE
.PP
Print the percentage of physical memory in use:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ free | awk \(aq/^Mem:/ { printf "%d%%\en", 100 * $3 / $2 }\(aq
32%
.EE
.RE
.PP
Show the memory in mebibytes every two seconds:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ watch \-n 2 free \-m
.EE
.RE
.SH NOTES
The figures are for the whole system.
.B free
gets physical memory and the system cache from
.BR GetPerformanceInfo ,
and the paging files from
.BR EnumPageFilesA ,
both in
.IR psapi.dll ,
which busybox loads when
.B free
runs.
It does not read
.IR /proc/meminfo .
.PP
Differences from
.B free
on Linux:
.IP \(bu 2
The only options are
.BR \-b ,
.BR \-k ,
.BR \-m ,
.BR \-g ,
.BR \-h ,
and
.BR \-\-help .
There is no
.BR \-t ", " \-s ", " \-c ", " \-l ", " \-w ", or " \-\-si ,
and no other long option.
.IP \(bu 2
The
.B shared
and
.B available
columns are always 0, so a script that reads
.B available
gets 0.
.IP \(bu 2
The extra
.B \-/+ buffers/cache
line, which busybox prints on Linux kernels that lack
.B MemAvailable
in
.IR /proc/meminfo ,
is never printed.
.PP
Output lines end in LF.
The output has no escape sequences, on a console or not.
.PP
In the x86 kit,
.B free
multiplies page counts by the page size in 32 bits, so a quantity of
4 GiB or more wraps around modulo 4 GiB.
On a machine with 4 GiB or more of memory, or of paging file space, the
figures are wrong.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR busybox (1),
.BR df (1),
.BR ps (1),
.BR uptime (1),
.BR watch (1)
